"id":"rt-s27","q":"State the treatment and prognosis stated for Wilms tumor.","a":["Nephrectomy.","Chemotherapy.","Radiotherapy.","Overall survival up to 90%."],"topic":"Wilms treatment","priority":"High","marks":4,"subtopics":["wilms-treatment"]},{"id":"rt-s28","q":"Summarize the epidemiology of UTUC.","a":["Forms 5%-10% of urothelial tumors.","Usually unilateral; up to 5% bilateral.","Pelvicalyceal tumors are twice as common as ureteral tumors.","Concurrent bladder cancer occurs in 17%.","Twice as frequent in men."],"topic":"UTUC epidemiology","priority":"High","marks":6,"subtopics":["utuc-epidemiology-risk"]},{"id":"rt-s29","q":"List the major risk factors stated for UTUC.","a":["Lynch syndrome (HNPCC) for hereditary disease.","Tobacco use, increasing relative risk 2.5-7 times."],"topic":"UTUC risk factors","priority":"High","marks":3,"subtopics":["utuc-epidemiology-risk"]},{"id":"rt-s30","q":"State the key diagnostic and treatment points of UTUC.","a":["CT urography has the highest diagnostic accuracy.","Cystoscopy should always be performed.","Flexible ureteroscopy with biopsy is a key diagnostic approach.","Treatment: nephroureterectomy with removal of a bladder cuff around the affected ureteric orifice."],"topic":"UTUC diagnosis and treatment","priority":"High","marks":6,"subtopics":["utuc-pathology-diagnosis","utuc-treatment"]}],"imageQuestions":[{"id":"rt-i01","title":"Gross renal cell carcinoma","topic":"RCC gross pathology","priority":"High","marks":5,"subtopics":["rcc-pathology"],"page":6,"prompt":"Inspect the gross renal specimen.","questions":["Identify the likely tumor.","State the characteristic color and its stated basis.","What type of capsule commonly surrounds RCC?"],"answer":["Renal cell carcinoma.","Yellow color due to high fat content.","A pseudocapsule of compressed renal parenchyma and fibrous tissue rather than a true histologic capsule."],"imageAsset":"rt-i01"},{"id":"rt-i02","title":"Contrast CT of a renal mass","topic":"RCC imaging","priority":"High","marks":4,"subtopics":["rcc-investigations"],"page":12,"prompt":"Review the two contrast-enhanced axial CT images.","questions":["Identify the imaging modality.","What renal abnormality is demonstrated?","State the lecture role of this study."],"answer":["Contrast-enhanced CT.","A renal mass consistent with the lecture section on RCC.","CT with contrast is used for diagnosis and staging."],"imageAsset":"rt-i02"},{"id":"rt-i03","title":"Child with a renal abdominal mass","topic":"Wilms presentation","priority":"High","marks":5,"subtopics":["wilms-clinical"],"page":17,"prompt":"The original labels have been masked. Use the clinical illustration to answer.","questions":["What diagnosis should be considered in a four-year-old with this finding?","List three associated clinical findings from the lecture.","What is the stated cause of hypertension?"],"answer":["Wilms tumor.","Abdominal pain or distention, anorexia, nausea or vomiting, hematuria and hypertension.","Renin production."],"imageAsset":"rt-i03"},{"id":"rt-i04","title":"Large pediatric renal mass on CT","topic":"Wilms imaging","priority":"High","marks":4,"subtopics":["wilms-investigations"],"page":20,"prompt":"Inspect the contrast CT image of a large renal-region mass in a child.","questions":["What is the likely diagnosis in the lecture context?","Which imaging test is primary, and which avoids ionizing radiation?"],"answer":["Wilms tumor.","Ultrasound is primary; MRI is preferred when avoiding ionizing radiation."],"imageAsset":"rt-i04"},{"id":"rt-i05","title":"Renal pelvis soft-tissue mass","topic":"UTUC imaging","priority":"High","marks":4,"subtopics":["utuc-pathology-diagnosis"],"page":27,"prompt":"Inspect the contrast-enhanced axial CT image and arrow.","questions":["Where is the soft-tissue mass located?","What diagnosis is suggested?","Which imaging study has the highest diagnostic accuracy for this disease?"],"answer":["Within the renal pelvis.","Upper urinary tract urothelial carcinoma.","CT urography."],"imageAsset":"rt-i05"},{"id":"rt-i06","title":"Goblet sign in the ureter","topic":"UTUC radiology","priority":"High","marks":4,"subtopics":["utuc-pathology-diagnosis"],"page":27,"prompt":"Inspect the retrograde contrast study and arrow.","questions":["Name the sign.","What diagnosis does it indicate in the lecture?"],"answer":["Goblet sign.","Transitional/urothelial carcinoma of the ureter."],"imageAsset":"rt-i06"}],"detailedShorts":[{"id":"rt-d01","q":"What are the three biological categories of renal masses?","a":["Malignant, benign and inflammatory."],"topic":"Classification","priority":"Core","marks":3,"subtopics":["classification"]},{"id":"rt-d02","q":"Name three benign renal masses.","a":["Renal cyst, angiomyolipoma and oncocytoma."],"topic":"Classification","priority":"Core","marks":3,"subtopics":["classification"]},{"id":"rt-d03","q":"Name three malignant renal masses.","a":["RCC, Wilms tumor and urothelial carcinoma."],"topic":"Classification","priority":"Core","marks":3,"subtopics":["classification"]},{"id":"rt-d04","q":"Name three inflammatory renal masses.","a":["Abscess, xanthogranulomatous pyelonephritis and tuberculosis."],"topic":"Classification","priority":"Core","marks":3,"subtopics":["classification"]},{"id":"rt-d05","q":"What are the radiographic appearance groups of renal masses?","a":["Simple cystic, complex cystic and solid."],"topic":"Classification","priority":"Core","marks":3,"subtopics":["classification"]},{"id":"rt-d06","q":"Why are asymptomatic RCCs increasingly detected?","a":["Because of widespread use of ultrasound and CT."],"topic":"RCC epidemiology","priority":"Core","marks":2,"subtopics":["rcc-epidemiology"]},{"id":"rt-d07","q":"What age group has peak RCC incidence?","a":["60-70 years."],"topic":"RCC epidemiology","priority":"Core","marks":1,"subtopics":["rcc-epidemiology"]},{"id":"rt-d08","q":"What is the male-to-female ratio in RCC?","a":["3:2."],"topic":"RCC epidemiology","priority":"Core","marks":1,"subtopics":["rcc-epidemiology"]},{"id":"rt-d09","q":"What is the inheritance pattern of VHL disease?","a":["Autosomal dominant."],"topic":"RCC genetics","priority":"Core","marks":1,"subtopics":["rcc-epidemiology"]},{"id":"rt-d10","q":"Which chromosome region contains the VHL mutation stated in the lecture?","a":["Chromosome 3p."],"topic":"RCC genetics","priority":"High","marks":1,"subtopics":["rcc-epidemiology"]},{"id":"rt-d11","q":"How does a first-degree relative affect RCC risk?","a":["It is associated with a significantly increased risk."],"topic":"RCC risk","priority":"Core","marks":2,"subtopics":["rcc-epidemiology"]},{"id":"rt-d12","q":"What histologic class are all RCCs?","a":["Adenocarcinomas derived from renal tubular epithelial cells."],"topic":"RCC pathology","priority":"High","marks":2,"subtopics":["rcc-pathology"]},{"id":"rt-d13","q":"What forms the RCC pseudocapsule?","a":["Compressed renal parenchyma and fibrous tissue."],"topic":"RCC pathology","priority":"Core","marks":2,"subtopics":["rcc-pathology"]},{"id":"rt-d14","q":"Which RCC subtype is most common?","a":["Clear cell RCC, accounting for 70%-80%."],"topic":"RCC histology","priority":"High","marks":2,"subtopics":["rcc-pathology"]},{"id":"rt-d15","q":"What percentage of RCC is papillary?","a":["10%-15%."],"topic":"RCC histology","priority":"Core","marks":1,"subtopics":["rcc-pathology"]},{"id":"rt-d16","q":"What percent